A||||2||maryjane||pastic rad tank repair-not a Fiero||||1||
Z||000000||maryjane||07-17-2001||08:13 PM||||My neighbor has a Mitsubishi and the plastic rad tank has developed a leak in the seam right on top of the tank. Is there anyway to have these repaired reliably? Pardon for posting a non-Fiero question here?||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||07-17-2001||08:30 PM||||Depending on the tank/leak, they can usually be repaired or retanked.<P>Before you fix it, call for prices on replacement ones from the parts stores. Then take the radiator to a repair shop and get an estimate. <P>If the estimate is more than half of a new one, get the new one. A new one will have a warantee on it. Repairs usually don't but ask the shop.<P>If the tank has cracked... depending on where the crack is... You might be able to try this yourself. I haven't looked at the glue market lately. If you do, make sure everything is absolutly clean and follow the directions on the adhesive carefully.||||reg||1
Z||000002||maryjane||07-17-2001||08:53 PM||||Thanks, Ogre. I'll pass this along. Might try to fix it if it were mine but don't want the liabilty for someone else's engine. She did say new one was $200. Cheap to me considering price of a cyl hd job or new eng.<BR>Don||||reg||1
